---
pagination:
  data: locales
  size: 1
  alias: locale
permalink: "/{{ locale }}/skills/"
layout: base.njk
extraCss:
  - https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/devicons/devicon@latest/devicon.min.css
  - /css/skills.css
{# Latest devicon CSS file loads a large .ttf font. 
    Interestingly, in version 2.7.0, the .woff is larger than the .ttf.
    Taking the .ttf and converting to .woff2 with fontsquirrel or something, 
    the file was smaller but not meaningfully. So not a trivial problem to solve.

    If I were to solve/optimize it, do it upstream. There is no advantage in a fork:
    at that point, just use SVGs! The whole advantage of using this is that I 
    never need to update anything when logos (e.g. Unity) change. 
    Sure, it's slow, but I always get latest logos with no updates ever on my part.
    And it looks perfectly fine if it never loads at all, vs an old logo looks unmaintained.
    The devicon font I'm loading above is from https://github.com/devicons/devicon 
    
    Some interesting but probably unnecessary info:
    https://api.github.com/repos/vorillaz/devicons - https://github.com/vorillaz/devicons
    https://api.github.com/repos/devicons/devicon - https://github.com/devicons/devicon 
    
    devicons/devicon was originally created by konpa according to the URL they link.
    Are these two packages forks? I don't know, konpa's is older but
    https://github.com/file-icons/DevOpicons credits vorillaz in its optimized fork.
    Unfortunately, both file-icons and vorillaz's versions are abandoned. 
    They load a much smaller .woff: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/devicons/1.8.0/css/devicons.min.css
    But kind of pointless if outdated versions of logos and missing newer stuff.
    Because again, just use SVGs at that point.
 #}

